      "meaning": "counting things",
      "explanation": "Japanese counts things by attaching a counter to the number, a bit like \"two sheets of paper\" or \"three bottles of water\" in English. 〜つ is the all-purpose counter for things up to nine (ten is とお): 一つ, 二つ, 三つ…. 〜枚 (まい) counts flat things — paper, stamps, shirts, plates. 〜本 (ほん) counts long, thin things — pens, bottles, umbrellas. Ask \"how many?\" with いくつ, 何枚 (なんまい) or 何本 (なんぼん).",
